## 1 Introduction

Neutron star-black hole (NSBH) mergers make up approximately two of the \\simeq 90 gravitational-wave events observed by the LIGO-Virgo-KAGRA (LVK) collaboration (Abbott et al., 2021; Abbott et al., 2021, 2021, 2021)1. While the first binary neutron star detection was a multi-messenger discovery (Abbott et al., 2017), neither of the NSBH events observed so far have been associated with a known electromagnetic counterpart. Observations of both binary neutron stars and NSBH systems can provide clues about the neutron star equation of state (e.g., Lindblom, 1992; Kochanek, 1992; Bejger et al., 2005; Flanagan & Hinderer, 2008; Read et al., 2009; Duez et al., 2010; Pannarale et al., 2011; Lattimer & Prakash, 2016; Oertel et al., 2017; Abbott et al., 2018). If the black hole is sufficiently light or spinning rapidly, the neutron star may tidally disrupt. Such a disruption could provide a progenitor for a gamma ray burst (e.g., Mochkovitch et al., 1993; Janka et al., 1999; Nakar, 2007), a kilonova (e.g., Li & Paczynski, 1998; Metzger et al., 2010; Kawaguchi et al., 2016), and the creation of r-process elements (e.g., Freiburghaus et al., 1999).

Footnote 1: We refer here to the two unambiguous NSBH detections described in Abbott et al. (2021). However up to four NSBH events may be included in the current catalog of events (Abbott et al., 2021).

When the neutron star in an NSBH disrupts, the gravitational-waveform cuts off abruptly. The frequency at which this occurs depends on the binary system parameters, including the equation of state of the neutron star (Vallisneri, 2000; Ferrari et al., 2010). The relationship between the neutron star equation of state and tidal disruption has been explored in numerical-relativity studies (e.g., Lackey et al., 2014; Foucart et al., 2013; Pannarale et al., 2015). However, most NSBH mergers are probably not associated with tidal disruption. A low mass ratio q=m_{2}/m_{1}\\lesssim 1/6, a high prograde black-hole spin (\\chi_{\\rm BH}\\gtrsim 0.5), and a large neutron-star radius are likely necessary ingredients for disruption (Kyutoku et al., 2011; Foucart et al., 2013; Hannam et al., 2013). Highly spinning black holes seem difficult to produce in most binary stellar evolution scenarios, since first-born black holes are thought to be non-spinning (e.g., Fuller & Ma, 2019), although spins may be induced through mass-transfer or Wolf-Rayet stellar winds (e.g., Steinle et al., 2023). This may suggest that some or most disrupting NSBH come from binaries that have undergone mass ratio reversal during their evolution; the neutron star is born first and the secondary star, which will go on to become a black hole, is spun up throughtidal interactions (Qin et al., 2018; Chattopadhyay et al., 2022; Hu et al., 2022). This scenario could account for up to 20% of the NSBH population (Broekgaarden et al., 2021).

We expect to detect gravitational waves from 1-180 NSBH mergers per year (Abadie et al., 2010; Baibhav et al., 2019; Broekgaarden et al., 2021) at LVK design sensitivity (Aasi et al., 2015; Acernese et al., 2015; Akutsu et al., 2020). However, the fraction of systems that will undergo tidal disruption will likely only comprise around 10% of this population (Kumar et al., 2017; Zappa et al., 2019; Zhu et al., 2021; Fragione, 2021; Biscoveanu et al., 2023). Even if disrupting binaries are a small minority of NSBH systems, future detectors such as Cosmic Explorer (Reitze et al., 2019) and Einstein Telescope (Maggiore et al., 2020) will allow us to observe a population of potentially disruptive binaries (e.g., Gupta et al., 2023).

There are several NSBH waveform approximants available that model the disruption of the neutron star. For example, Lackey et al. (2014); Thompson et al. (2020); Matas et al. (2020); Gonzalez et al. (2022) all present waveform models tuned to numerical-relativity simulations of NSBH binaries. They include prescriptions to damp the gravitational waveform at the onset of tidal disruption. In these models, the disruption time is determined by the neutron star equation of state. The mass and spin of the remnant black holes are informed by the models developed by Pannarale (2013, 2014) and later Zappa et al. (2019). These models classify the mergers according to the descriptions of Pannarale et al. (2015, 2015) as disruptive, non-disruptive, and mildly disruptive to further improve the waveform accuracy when compared to numerical relativity.

In this Letter we show that the disruption of a nearby NSBH system can be measured in gravitational-wave data. We show that this can be used to constrain the neutron star radius, though this constraint is wider than the constraint obtained from measuring the neutron star tidal deformability parameter during the inspiral. The remainder of this Letter is organised as follows. In Section 2 we describe a phenomenological model for the gravitational-wave signal from a disrupting NSBH. In Section 3 we describe our analysis of a simulated NSBH signal. In Section 4 we show that, while we can measure the disruption time of the system to within \\lesssim 1\\,ms, this measurement does not translate to a superior constraint on the neutron star radius compared to measuring the tidal deformability during the inspiral. We show that the tidal deformability measurement provides a more precise constraint on the radius and therefore the equation of state. Moreover, NSBH disruptions provide less information about the neutron-star equation of state than what we will learn from measurements of tidal effects with merging binary neutron stars.

## 2 Disrupting NSBH waveform model

For the waveform models mentioned above, the disruption time is determined by the binary parameters and the assumed neutron star equation of state. In this analysis, we seek to determine the extent to which the disruption can be "seen," and how much information is provided by the observation of the disruption versus the observation of tidal effects. To this end, we treat the disruption time as an independent parameter, even though in reality, it is determined by the other binary parameters and the equation of state. This allows us to answer questions like: "with what precision can we measure the disruption time?" which we could not ask if the disruption time was already determined by the measurement of binary parameters obtained from the inspiral.

We use the binary neutron star waveform IMRPhenomPv2_NRTidal (Dietrich et al., 2019), denoted h_{\\rm BNS}(t), as a starting point to construct a phenomenological model of a disrupting NSBH system. We also treat the neutron-star tidal deformability as a free parameter. We also sample over the tidal deformability of the primary object, which we model as a black hole with tidal deformability \\Lambda_{1}=0. Using these assumptions, we obtain a preliminary IMRPhenomPv2_NRTidal waveform. We multiply this waveform by a hyperbolic tangent "window" function w(t|\\tau_{d},\\Delta t), inspired by the amplitude corrections employed by Lackey et al. (2014); Thompson et al. (2020); Matas et al. (2020), which simulates a disruption by prematurely terminating the waveform:

h(t)=h_{\\rm BNS}(t)\\,w(t|\\tau_{d},\\Delta t),  where

w(t|\\tau_{d},\\Delta t)=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\tanh\\Big{(}\\frac{2(\\tau_{d}+ \\Delta t-t)}{\\Delta t}\\Big{)}. 

Here, \\tau_{d} is a free parameter describing the time of disruption relative to the merger time of the original IMRPhenomPv2_NRTidal waveform. Meanwhile, 2\\Delta t is a free parameter describing the time interval over which the strain goes to zero. Figure 1 shows an example tidal-disruption waveform (teal) alongside the original IMRPhenomPv2_NRTidal waveform (pink).

## 3 Analysis of a simulated NSBH signal

We inject a tidally disrupting NSBH signal into the simulated Gaussian noise of two 40 km Cosmic Explorer detectors (Abbott et al., 2017) located at the sites of LIGO Hanford and LIGO Livingston.2. We optimistically choose binary parameters (shown in Table 1) to achieve a tidal disruption at approximately the lowest realistic frequency given our understanding of compact objects: \\approx 1600\\,Hz. In order to achieve this low frequency, the black hole has a mass of only 3.6M_{\\odot} with a high prograde spin of \\chi_{1}=0.9. We assume aligned spins in this study. The 1.2M_{\\odot} neutron star has a dimensionless tidal deformability of \\Lambda_{2}=960 corresponding to a radius of 12\\,\\mathrm{km}. This is a relatively stiff choice of equation of state given that Abbott et al. (2018) constrains \\Lambda_{1.4}=190^{+390}_{-120} and R_{1.4}=11.9^{+1.4}_{-1.4}\\,\\mathrm{km} with the binary neutron star merger GW170817 but is within the constraints calculated using NICER mass and radius measurements of pulsars (Miller et al., 2021; Legred et al., 2021). Appendix A provides further details on our choice of parameters. We optimistically place the merger at a distance of 150\\,\\mathrm{Mpc}, which is closer than both of the NSBH events observed by the LVK (Abbott et al., 2021). This corresponds to an optimal network signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) of \\approx 335 in our Cosmic Explorer network. We use Bilby (Ashton et al., 2019; Romero-Shaw et al., 2020) and Parallel_Bilby (Smith et al., 2020) to perform Bayesian inference on a simulated NSBH system. In order to reduce the computational challenge of this calculation, and because we are primarily interested in the disruption and tides of the system, we begin our analysis at 100\\,\\mathrm{Hz} and only analyse the final 12\\,\\mathrm{s} of the signal up to a frequency of 2048\\,\\mathrm{Hz}. We fix some parameters to their injected values, which Cosmic Explorer is predicted to be able to measure extremely accurately during the inspiral for such a nearby source: m_{1}, m_{2}, RA, Dec (e.g., Vitale & Evans, 2017; Vitale & Whittle, 2018; Smith et al., 2021; Borhanian & Sathyaprakash, 2022; Iacovelli et al., 2022). Iacovelli et al. (2022) forecast the precision with which different parameters can be measured for NSBH in a three-detector third-generation network. They predict that at SNR 300: the chirp mass can be measured with a fractional uncertainty of \\approx 10^{-5}, the symmetric mass ratio can be measured with a precision of \\approx 10^{-4} and the sky position can be measured with a precision of \\approx 0.1\\mathrm{deg}^{2}. We fix the duration of the tidal disruption \\Delta t=2\\,\\mathrm{ms}, based on numerical relativity studies that show the gravitational-wave amplitude decaying on the timescale of \\approx\\mathrm{few}\\,\\mathrm{ms}(e.g., Yamamoto et al., 2008; Shibata et al., 2009; Chaurasia et al., 2021). We sample over the remaining parameters

\\Theta=\\{\\chi_{1},\\chi_{2},\\phi_{c},\\theta_{\\mathrm{IN}},\\psi,\\Lambda_{1}, \\Lambda_{2},t_{\\mathrm{geo}},\\tau_{d}\\}. 

Table 1 shows the injection parameters and priors used for our simulation. We use the nested sampler dynesty(Speagle, 2019) to obtain posterior samples for \\Theta.

There is a one-to-one mapping between disruption time \\tau_{d} and neutron-star radius R_{\\mathrm{NS}}. First, we map the disruption time to disruption frequency using the functional definition of the IMRPhenomPv2_NRTidal phase as implemented in LalSimulation (LIGO Scientific Collaboration, 2018; Garcia-Quiros et al., 2020). The derivative of the phase as a function of time gives the gravitational-wave frequency as a function of time. Next, we solve for the neutron-star compactness using the "EOS independent" fitting formula from Pannarale et al. (2015), which relates the compactness to the disruption frequency in dimensionless units:

f_{\\mathrm{cut}}=\\sum_{i,j,k=0}^{3}f_{ijk}\\,C^{i}\\,q^{-j}\\,\\chi^{k}  where

i+j+k\\leq 3. 

