## Appendix A Choice of simulation parameters

We choose our system parameters such that the disruption frequency is minimised for a realistic disrupting binary. Figure 4 shows the disruption frequency as a function of mass ratio for an NSBH with m_{\\rm NS}=1.2\\,\\rm M_{\\odot}, R_{\\rm NS}=12\\,\\rm km and \\chi_{\\rm BH}=0.9. We find that a mass ratio of q\\approx 1/3 provides the optimal conditions to resolve the neutron star disruption given a fixed neutron-star mass of m_{\\rm NS}=1.2M_{\\odot}, a black-hole spin of \\chi_{\\rm BH}=0.9, and a neutron-star radius of R_{\\rm NS}=12\\,\\rm km. We verify this by testing a system with the same parameters (m_{\\rm NS},\\chi_{\\rm BH},R_{\\rm NS}) but a mass ratio of 0.2. In other words, we increase the black hole mass while keeping all other parameters fixed. On the one hand, increasing the black-hole mass increases the overall strain amplitude, which increases the optimal SNR. On the other hand, it increases the disruption frequency, pushing it into the shot noise where it is difficult to resolve (e.g., Brown et al., 2022).5 The loss of SNR from the increased disruption frequency is the more important effect. Systems with mass ratios of 0.2 or less are likely more abundant in nature than those with larger mass ratios (e.g., Biscoveanu et al., 2023). The neutron star radius posterior distributions for this run are shown in Figure 5.

Footnote 5: It also causes the tidal deformability to be less resolvable because the post-Newtonian expression that introduces tides includes the mass ratio (e.g., Lackey et al., 2014; Dietrich et al., 2019; Coupechoux et al., 2022).

